{
  "name": "What to Expect from a Detailed Roof Condition Report in Argyle",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1: Initial Consultation and History Review",
      "text": "Before the physical inspection, the professional will likely discuss the recent storm event, any specific concerns you have, and your property's roof history. They'll want to know about previous repairs or any existing issues. This helps them understand the context before they even step on your roof. This phase usually takes about 15-30 minutes, and it's a conversation between you and the inspector to set the stage.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 2: Ground-Level and Roof-Level Inspection",
      "text": "This is where the real work happens. The inspector will first check your roof from the ground, looking for obvious signs of damage. Then, where safe, they'll perform a thorough roof-level inspection. They'll examine shingles, flashing, valleys, ridge caps, gutters, penetrations, and ventilation components. They'll also check related interior or attic areas if accessible and relevant. This hands-on inspection can take anywhere from 30 minutes to a few hours, depending on the roof's size and complexity. The inspector will be documenting everything with photos and notes as they go.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 2
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 3: Documentation and Damage Identification",
      "text": "During the inspection, the professional will be meticulously photographing each finding. Crucially, they'll distinguish between damage caused by the storm (like hail impact or wind lift) and normal wear and tear due to age. Each photo should have directional or location references to make it clear where the issue is on your roof. This documentation is for their report and your records, and it happens concurrently with the physical inspection.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 3
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 4: Written Report Generation",
      "text": "After the inspection, the professional will compile all their findings into a dated, written report. This report should clearly outline the observed damage, specify the affected roof sections, include all the photographic evidence, and provide measurements where necessary. It will also note any limitations due to unsafe or inaccessible conditions. You should expect this report to be delivered within a few days of the inspection, summarizing all the details and recommending next steps for repair or replacement.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 4
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 5: Recommendations and Discussion",
      "text": "The report will conclude with clear repair recommendations based on the findings. While the inspector assesses the physical condition, they generally won't determine insurance coverage. They'll discuss their findings with you, explain the recommended actions, and answer any questions you have. This final discussion helps you understand your options and plan confidently. This can be a separate meeting or a call, typically within a day or two of you receiving the written report. For a truly detailed roof condition report in Argyle, trust the experts at Discounted Roofing Co.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 5
    }
  ],
  "@type": "HowTo",
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "speakable": {
    "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
    "cssSelector": [
      "h1",
      ".howto-step",
      "[itemprop=\"step\"]"
    ]
  },
